Service type inheritance (X-KDE-Derived)
========================================
X-KDE-Derived is for service types. ST1 derives from ST2 means ST1 is more specific than ST2.
If a service implements ST1, it also implements ST2, but not the other way round.

For instance, KoDocument derives from KParts/ReadWritePart, which derives from KParts/ReadOnlyPart,
so KWord's part (which implements KoDocument) can be used as a readonly viewer for KWord files
in Konqueror (which looks for a KParts/ReadOnlyPart).

Mimetype "inheritance"
======================
We need a mechanism to also say that "text/xml is a special case of text/plain",
or "text/docbook is a kind of text/sgml", or "application/x-smb-workgroup is a kind
of inode/directory", etc. See below.

Why mimetype "inheritance" doesn't use X-KDE-Derived
====================================================
The confusing thing is that we said "a mimetype is a servicetype". But that's not exactly correct.
As Waldo noted, "the ability to open a mimetype" is what's a servicetype.
So if text/xml said X-KDE-Derived=text/plain (i.e. ST1=text/xml, ST2=text/plain),
then an application opening text/xml (ST1) could also open text/plain (ST2) - which is not necessarily true.

We want the other way round: to be able to open special kinds of text/plain in a plain text editor.
This is what X-KDE-IsAlso was introduced for. X-KDE-IsAlso kind of "works the other way" than X-KDE-Derived.
If M1 is a special kind of M2 (mimetypes), then "the ability to open M2" derives from "the ability to open M1"
So we say in text/xml:  X-KDE-IsAlso=text/plain, and applications that can open text/plain can also open text/xml.

Pattern Accuracy
======================
In some cases of inheritance several mimetypes shares the same extension (with the primary case being Ogg Multimedia files).
This has been solved sofar by matching the extension to most generic mimetype. To indicate that the mimetype can be refined further, the flag X-KDE-PatternsAccuracy can be set. The value to X-KDE-PatternsAccuracy is a number between 0-100, with 100 being the default if not set. Any values below 100 is interpreted as the possibility of further mimetype refinement.

freedesktop.org shared-mime-info
================================
kbuildsycoca also reads the MIME database of shared-mime-info ($XDG_DATA_HOME/mime and
$XDG_DATA_DIRS/mime: the "xdgdata-mime" resource), so that the types other programs install
(KiCad, FreeCAD, ...) are known, and the MimeType= lines of their .desktop files work.

* It reads the per-type files update-mime-database generates (mime/<media>/<subtype>.xml):
  one file per type, like the mimelnk files, so the incremental update and kded's directory
  watching work as for those. They hold the globs with weight and case sensitivity, aliases,
  sub-class-of, icon, generic-icon and the translated comments. The magic rules are read at
  run time by KMimeMagic, from the generated binary mime/magic file.
* The KDE mimelnk files win: a type KDE defines keeps its patterns, icon, comment and
  X-KDE-* keys; shared-mime-info only adds the glob patterns no KDE type claims.
  A shared-mime-info type that KDE knows under an alias (application/zip, KDE's
  application/x-zip), or under another name listed in kbuildservicetypefactory.cpp
  (application/x-compressed-tar is KDE's application/x-tgz) becomes an alias of the KDE type.
  The other types become KMimeType entries of their own (X-KDE-SharedMimeInfo=true).
  The copies of a type's file in several directories are merged (the more important
  one wins for the comment and icons; <glob-deleteall/> drops the globs of the others).
* Aliases: KMimeType::mimeType(), KServiceType::serviceType() and the MimeType= lines of
  services resolve them (and the lowercased names: audio/amr-wb is audio/AMR-WB) through
  an alias table in the servicetype factory. Applications that name a KDE type by an
  alias come after those naming it directly, so KDE's default applications stay.
* Parents: X-KDE-IsAlso holds the first sub-class-of type, X-KDE-ParentMimeTypes all of them;
  KMimeType::parentMimeTypes() and is() follow them. Every text/* type is a text/plain.
  The applications offered for a shared-mime-info type itself come before those offered
  for its parents (KiCad before the text editors for a .kicad_pcb).
* Globs: the lowercased file name is tried against the shared-mime-info globs that are
  not case-sensitive when the name itself matches no pattern, the longest "*.suffix" wins, and the patterns with
  '?' or '[...]' are matched as such. When shared-mime-info types share a pattern with the
  same weight, one of them gets it with X-KDE-PatternsAccuracy=50, so the contents decide;
  magic that finds only a parent of the type (text/plain) keeps the type of the name.
* Magic: the shared-mime-info rules are only consulted when KDE's own magic found nothing
  more than text/plain or application/octet-stream; for text, a rule must be a text type of
  priority 40 or more, or of priority 80 or more. The rules of a directory replace those
  of the less important directories for the same type; a section with a line that can't
  be read is dropped whole. The rules are read once per process.
* Icons: the type's icon, the freedesktop.org default name (application-x-kicad-pcb) or
  its generic icon, whichever the icon theme (hicolor included) has; otherwise the KDE 3
  icon for the generic one (text-x-generic is "txt"), or the parent type's icon.
